    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class and both its tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the create/update owner flow, keeping the flat `address` fully backward-compatible.\n\n**What changed and why it's low-impact** \u2014 the address logic lives in one new, isolated helper so the existing functions barely change:\n\n1. **`StructuredAddress.java`** (new) \u2014 the only place holding the new complexity (WMC_other = 0). It normalizes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` via the existing `NormaliseAddress`, prefers the structured fields when `addressLine1` is non-blank, and composes `address` = normalized `addressLine1` + `\" \"` + normalized `addressLine2` (the second part only when present). When `addressLine1` is absent it just normalizes the flat `address`. Either path yields a non-blank `address` exactly when the request supplied one.\n\n2. **`RequireOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 one line changed: the old single-field normalize call now delegates to `StructuredAddress.apply(request)`. The existing \"address blank?\" check then transparently enforces the \"valid with either form\" rule, so validation stays backward-compatible.\n\n3. **`ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT path) \u2014 routes through the same helper and copies the two new fields, keeping update consistent with create.\n\n4. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` columns + accessors so the structured fields persist and round-trip. MapStruct maps them by name (create via `toOwner`, response via `toOwnerDto`) with no mapper edits.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` to `OwnerFields` (so both the request and response DTOs gain them) and dropped `address` from `required` so a structured-only request validates.\n\n6. **hsqldb `schema.sql` / `data.sql`** \u2014 appended the two nullable columns at the end of `owners` (so existing positional seed inserts keep their meaning) and added the matching trailing NULLs to the seed rows. Only the hsqldb schema is maintained for this evolved app (the h2/postgres/mysql schemas already lack `postcode`, `customer_code`, etc.), and it's the one the tests use.\n\nThe household hash, postcode validation and locality in this codebase key off `postcode`/`city` (not the address string), so they already read structured fields and needed no change.",
